The strongest way to present it is not as “a certificate expired,” but as:

A production CI/CD outage caused by incomplete certificate renewal across dependent load balancer endpoints, followed by RCA, recovery, and preventive monitoring/process improvements.

Below are interview-ready notes.

1. 30-Second Incident Summary
If the interviewer asks:

“Tell me about a production incident you handled.”

You can say:

“We had a major production incident where GitLab CI/CD pipelines started failing because GitLab Kubernetes runners were unable to pull a Docker-in-Docker image from our private Nexus Docker registry.

The error was an x509: certificate has expired error while establishing the HTTPS connection to the private Docker registry.

During the RCA, we found that although the certificate had been renewed, the renewed certificate had not been attached to the load balancer serving the Nexus subdomain and external vendor endpoints. As a result, those endpoints were still presenting the expired certificate.

We corrected the certificate configuration on the relevant load balancer, after which image pulls and CI/CD pipelines recovered.

As preventive actions, we updated the certificate renewal checklist to verify certificate attachment across all associated load balancers and endpoints. We also identified a monitoring gap and proposed Elastic synthetic/heartbeat monitoring and proactive certificate expiry alerts at 30, 15, and 7 days.”

That's a very strong opening.

